// §10 third-party schema survey — SpiceDB's own "superuser / site-wide
// permissions" example (a deliberately-designed root/backdoor role).
// Source (fetched verbatim):
// https://raw.githubusercontent.com/authzed/examples/main/schemas/superuser/schema-and-data.yaml
// README (fetched verbatim, same directory): "Access is granted to all
// resources of a specific type to a super-admin user." The fixture's own
// assertTrue (`document:lasers#admin@user:drevil`, reached only via
// platform:evilempire's administrator) confirms this backdoor is
// intended behavior, not a bug in the source.
//
// Disclosed restructuring: the source's `owner: user | organization`
// followed by `owner + owner->admin` relies on SpiceDB silently skipping
// subject types that don't define the traversed permission (`user` has
// no `admin`). This compiler requires every type in a traversed relation
// to declare the target, so `owner` is split by type into `owner_user`
// and `owner_org` — semantically identical, since `owner_user` alone
// (with no further arrow) is exactly what the original's `user`-typed
// owner tuples contributed.

namespace document {
  relation owner_user: user
  relation owner_org: organization
  permission admin = owner_user | owner_org | owner_org->admin
}

namespace organization {
  relation platform: platform
  permission admin = platform->super_admin
}

namespace platform {
  relation administrator: user
  permission super_admin = administrator
}
